{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Implementation brief — error-code remediation stack (all decisions RESOLVED)\n\nThe audit is complete and every decision is made. This epic is ready to execute as ONE coordinated held-out-TDD Gerrit stack. Authoritative principle and per-child plan:\n\n### Unified principle (the one honest rule)\nReserve **`llm_unavailable`** for genuine availability faults only — `isinstance(exc, LLMUnavailableError)` (incl. `LLMConfigError`): extra absent, credentials missing/invalid, provider unreachable/rate-limited/refused. Route **every other `LLMError` subclass** to the existing broad-but-accurate **`command_failed`** (\"an unclassified rebar command failure\") — it makes no false cause claim. **Discriminate by exception TYPE, not by phase.** Do NOT invent finer codes (input_rejected on the *error-code* vocabulary, output_invalid, …) unless a consumer demonstrably needs the distinction — grow vocabulary only on observable need (35ab/551b precedent).\n\n### Per-child plan and stack order (bottom → top of the relation chain)\n1. **f75f** (core): narrow `error_code_for` branch 6 in `src/rebar/_errors.py` to map only `LLMUnavailableError` → `llm_unavailable`; all other `LLMError` → `command_failed`. This single change also **resolves 7d47** (WorkflowError family) and the 73d8/551b tension (a genuine execute-time outage is an `LLMUnavailableError` and stays `llm_unavailable`; an `ExpressionError` is not → `command_failed`).\n2. **fbc0**: `_structured_llm_failure` (the 4 MCP gate tools) — stop hardcoding `error=llm_unavailable`; call `error_code_for(exc)` so the type discrimination applies uniformly.\n3. **7d47**: add the observable test proving `render_workflow` MCP tool now surfaces the WorkflowError family as `command_failed`, not `llm_unavailable` (rides f75f; assert through the tool's JSON).\n4. **0aee**: register `pinned_ticket_read_failed` in `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` (vocabulary-contract gap — the code is emitted but unregistered).\n5. **5f96** (Option B, operator-approved): split the overloaded `coverage.llm_unavailable` boolean in `gate_dispatch.py` — keep it True only for real outages; add a distinct `input_rejected` signal for `LLMInputRejectedError`; update the 3 readers (`plan_review/drift_floor.py:202`, `plan_review/orchestrator.py:468`, `review_bot/adapter.py:114`). Do NOT add a broad `degraded` category (explicitly rejected by the operator).\n\n### Epic-level prevention guardrail (acceptance criterion for the epic)\nAdd an **exhaustive `exception → code` contract test** over `LLMError.__subclasses__()` (every subclass maps to the intended code, asserted by type), plus a **vocabulary-closure test** (every code any producer can emit ∈ `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` — this pins 0aee and prevents future unregistered codes). These are the regression oracles that make the whole class un-regressable.\n\n### Discipline\nHeld-out TDD: behavioral tests assert the observable error code **through the real surface** (CLI exit + JSON, MCP tool JSON), RED-for-right-reason first; implementer sees happy-path only. Land as a Gerrit relation-chain stack in the order above; close children bottom-up; each change carries a `rebar-ticket:` trailer + DCO. No `--force` anywhere; gates run unbounded."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788059937124677968,"uuid":"f32190a7-6c8f-438a-9886-7aee67e4c98b"}